---
/**
 * /radar/campus/sources - the Source Manager.
 *
 * An honest inventory of what Radar can and cannot read, including the things
 * it deliberately does not. `unavailable` is a first-class status, distinct
 * from `failed`, and the reason is stated in full: a source that silently
 * returns zero looks exactly like a quiet week, which is the worst possible
 * failure mode for a discovery tool.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import { campus, fetchedAt, research } from '@/lib/data.ts';

const all = [...research.sources, ...campus.sources];

const statusOrder = { failed: 0, degraded: 1, unavailable: 2, ok: 3 } as const;
const sorted = [...all].sort(
  (a, b) => statusOrder[a.status] - statusOrder[b.status] || b.itemCount - a.itemCount,
);

const totalItems = all.reduce((sum, source) => sum + source.itemCount, 0);
const healthy = all.filter((source) => source.status === 'ok').length;

/** Sources Radar knows about but does not read, with the reason. */
const notIngested = [
  {
    label: 'Gmail (a CampusRadar label)',
    why: 'Designed but not built. The privacy-first shape is the right one — the user creates a `CampusRadar` Gmail label, filters route newsletters into it, and Radar reads only that label — but it needs OAuth and a server to hold the token, and Radar is a static site with no backend.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Instagram',
    why: 'Public-content access is restricted to Business/Creator accounts and hashtag search is capped at 30 unique hashtags per rolling seven days. Architecting a feed around that would break the first time a flyer-posting org changed account type.',
  },
  {
    label: 'X',
    why: 'The developer platform is consumption-priced. A small curated account watchlist would be viable; it is not free, so it is a decision rather than an oversight.',
  },
  {
    label: 'YouTube',
    way: true,
    why: 'The Data API is good and watching official department channels would work. Not yet wired up — it is queued behind sources that carry events rather than recordings.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Sources — Radar"
  description="What Radar reads, what it cannot read, and why."
  engine="campus"
  fetchedAt={fetchedAt}
  current="sources"
>
  <section class="standfirst">
    <h1>Sources</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {healthy} of {all.length} sources healthy on the last run, contributing {totalItems} raw records
      before deduplication. None of these are read in your browser — every upstream is fetched at
      build time, because not one of them sends CORS headers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Last run</h2>
    <div class="panel table-scroll">
      <table class="table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Source</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">Records</th>
            <th scope="col">Time</th>
            <th scope="col">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            sorted.map((source) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {source.docsUrl.length > 0 ? (
                    <a href={source.docsUrl} rel="noopener external">
                      {source.label}
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    source.label
                  )}
                  <br />
                  <span style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--muted);">{source.vertical}</span>
                </th>
                <td>
                  <span class:list={['status', `status-${source.status}`]}>{source.status}</span>
                </td>
                <td>{source.itemCount}</td>
                <td>{(source.durationMs / 1000).toFixed(1)}s</td>
                <td style="font-size:0.8125rem; color:var(--muted);">
                  {source.failedRequests > 0 && (
                    <strong style="display:block; color:var(--updated-fg);">
                      {source.failedRequests} request(s) failed — items it could not deliver were kept
                      from the previous run rather than dropped
                    </strong>
                  )}
                  {source.note ?? (source.failedRequests > 0 ? '' : '—')}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Known, but not ingested</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Every one of these is a decision with a reason, not a gap someone forgot about.
    </p>
    <div class="panel prose">
      {
        notIngested.map((entry) => (
          <>
            <h3>{entry.label}</h3>
            <p>{entry.why}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>How Radar behaves as a guest</h2>
    <div class="panel prose">
      <p>
        Several of these APIs are run by public institutions and owe this project nothing, so the
        HTTP layer is built around not being a nuisance:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A descriptive User-Agent</strong> with a contact URL on every request. Anonymous
          scraping is how an IP gets blocked, and the block lands on a shared runner.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Per-host rate limiting.</strong> NCBI allows 3 requests/second per IP without an
          API key; arXiv's manual asks for one request every three seconds. Both are enforced by
          hostname, so the budget holds across every caller in a run rather than per connector.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bounded retries</strong> with exponential backoff and jitter, and
          <code>Retry-After</code> honoured when a server sends it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One pass per build.</strong> Radar makes tens of requests per run, not thousands,
          and nothing is fetched on page view.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>When a source cannot be reached</h3>
      <p>
        A failed request does not delete anything. If every source that knows about an item was
        unreachable on a given run, the item is carried forward from the previous snapshot and left
        marked unchanged — because it never went anywhere, and announcing a two-year-old paper as
        "new" because a server was slow would undermine the one badge this whole feed rests on.
        Items that a <em>healthy</em> source simply stopped returning are dropped, which is what
        should happen when an event passes or a listing is withdrawn.
      </p>

      <h3>What Radar refuses to publish</h3>
      <p>
        The Texas A&amp;M calendar includes a staff email on most events with registration, and the
        study registry includes a coordinator address on nearly every listing. Radar drops those at
        the connector, strips any address that appears in free text, and the build fails if an email
        address reaches the published output. None of them are needed to tell you an event exists.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
